Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

Rain patters against the floor-to-ceiling glass, blurring the gray Seattle skyline. 
(SOUND: Rain patters against the glass)

NARRATOR (30s, fit, wearing a crisp business casual shirt) stands by the window. He holds a steaming mug of coffee.

NARRATOR
Big brother was mine. I’m not talking about that stupid book we had to read in junior high. No, I’m talking about my real Big Brother. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Heavy iron plates crash together. The air smells of sweat and chalk.
(SOUND: Clanking of heavy iron weights)

NICK (20s, massive build, reddish-brown hair, face scarred like a puzzle) marches toward a curl bench. 

A muscular gym-goer sees Nick coming, drops his 40-pound bar, and scurries away. Nick nods, loads a 50-pound bar, and starts curling effortlessly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. When he walked in the bar, or the gym, people took notice, and man, did they move out of the way.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Street traffic hums along the Milwaukee sidewalk. 
(SOUND: Street traffic, a bicycle bell rings)

Narrator (20s, skinny, wearing a flour-dusted pizza parlor uniform) walks toward the entrance. 

SIONNA (20s, short brown hair, pale skin, wearing a V-neck shirt and a fish necklace) trips on the uneven cement. She falls, her shoulder brushing hard against Narrator.

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt. Her eyes are a piercing, watery light blue.

NARRATOR
I work here, I’m just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That’s different. I never heard of that. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. The Matron of the River Shannon.

NARRATOR
I asked if you wanted to come in and have some pizza.

SIONNA
No thanks, I don’t eat pizza, but if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She walks away, moving smoothly, like a wave. She looks back over her shoulder once.

INT. APARTMENT - NIGHT

A modest fifth-floor walk-up. Faded wallpaper. 
(SOUND: Television plays a loud game show)

MA (60s, brown hair, frail, wearing a worn afghan) stands at the kitchen counter. She stares blankly at a row of pill bottles.

NARRATOR
Ma, what’s the matter?

MA
I can’t find my pain pills, I know I put them here somewhere. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma, what are you talking about? We don’t go to church anymore. Mrs. Canasta lives down one level. Your pills are right in front of you.

Ma's eyes are glassy and empty. She blinks, snapping out of it.

MA
Oh, right, I am so sorry. I see them right here. I don’t know what got into me.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College students hunch over laptops. 
(SOUND: Espresso machine hisses loudly)

Narrator sits at a small table with Sionna. She wears a loose green top with a hood.

SIONNA
The cycle of life is ever-changing. We have to be prepared to change with it, to be willing to let go of things, and also accept things that happen to us, without grasping onto them.

NARRATOR
I don’t get that. I’m sitting in a chair, I’m breathing air, and I’m looking at you. How could all this be just fake?

SIONNA
It’s not fake, it’s just not something that will last for more than the present moment. Once it was a tree, now it’s a chair, and someday it will just be rotted and return to the earth.

NARRATOR
You make me feel smarter just by being around you.

SIONNA
Love is the universal language, and we know how to speak it.

She reaches across the table and takes his hand.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

The front door flies open. Nick stomps inside, his face beet red, fists clenching and unclenching.
(SOUND: Heavy boots stomp on the hardwood floor)

Ma sits in her chair, staring at the TV, oblivious.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you? Don’t you remember your responsibilities around here?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I’m sorry, I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? What the hell is that? With who?

NARRATOR
I met a girl, and we had a couple dates. That’s all.

NICK
Oh, great. A girl. That’s worth all this. Don’t you think Ma deserves better than that? Try looking at your priorities Bro, you just might learn something.

NARRATOR
I said I was sorry!

Nick turns and walks out, slamming the door behind him.

INT. DOCTOR'S OFFICE - DAY

Sterile white walls. 
(SOUND: Distant ambulance siren wails)

DR. CARSON (50s, wearing a white coat) stands holding a clipboard. Narrator sits nervously.

DR. CARSON
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. It’s so high, I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke. My advice is that you take her directly to the emergency room.

NARRATOR
Whoa man. I can’t believe this. Are you sure it’s that bad?

DR. CARSON
Your mother has been confused, is that right? And she also experienced chest pressure this afternoon?

NARRATOR
Yeah, that’s right. She hasn’t been acting normal lately.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Crowded, smoky, neon signs buzzing. 
(MUSIC: Heavy rock music blares from the jukebox)
(SOUND: Pool balls clack sharply)

Nick stands aggressively in front of LINDSEY (20s, blonde hair, pink sweater). Beside her is a DARK HAIRED MAN (20s, protective stance).

NICK
Where did you meet this one? I didn’t hear of you dating anybody.

LINDSEY
Nick, why does it matter to you?

DARK HAIRED MAN
What the fuck is wrong with you? You got a problem with me? Hell, I never even seen you before, and you’re in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's nostrils flare. His fists clench. The room goes dead silent except for the jukebox.

Narrator steps directly between Nick and the Dark Haired Man.

NARRATOR
Nick, Nick, listen — you don’t have any problem with this guy. You don’t even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it man, just leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! It’s none of your Goddamn business. You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face asshole.

NARRATOR
Stop it! Stop it! You’re crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego Nick!

Nick draws his right fist back. Lindsey screams. 

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

The bartender and a bouncer tackle Nick from behind. Narrator turns and runs out the door.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

An old duplex living room. A lumpy chocolate-colored couch. 
(SOUND: Wind rattles the old window panes)

Narrator sits on the couch, shaking. Sionna strokes the side of his face.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR
Yes, he did. I love Nick though, I mean, he’s my brother. He has always taken care of us.

SIONNA
He is a violent, out-of-control person right now. You have learned to use your brain. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can’t let him intimidate you, or stop you from your purpose.

NARRATOR
My purpose... what is my purpose?

SIONNA
The purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it. To be the best person you can while you’re here.

INT. APARTMENT - DAY

Ma sits in her chair, looking happy. Sionna sets two plates of peach pie on the coffee table.
(SOUND: Forks scrape lightly against ceramic plates)

The door opens. Nick walks in, wearing his brown suede jacket. He stops, eyeing Sionna suspiciously.

MA
Oh My God! It’s you Nick! I thought we would never see you again.

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you.

She holds out her hand. Nick shakes it reluctantly.

NICK
So, I hear you’re a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I am thinking college professor, if I can get my student teaching done.

NICK
Well, professor, I hope the best for ya. It’s even harder for a woman to teach at that level, or so I’ve heard. If you ask me, that might be one of the reasons this country is heading down the flippin’ tubes. The women in this country can’t stay home and take care of their kids.

SIONNA
I have to disagree with you there. Women in the area of education are adding other dimensions to the learning environment. Women bring different ways of thinking to the table. Diversity helps us learn. After all, we are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

Sionna smiles politely and walks into the kitchen. Nick sits stunned, his face boiling red.

NICK
(whispering to Narrator)
Sounds like you two have it all figured out, with help of course from Doctor Sionna. Take care of Ma.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

An ancient stone building. A busy front desk.
(SOUND: Telephones ring incessantly in the background)

Narrator sits in a hard plastic chair, holding a styrofoam cup of water. His hands shake. 

OFFICER DUPUIS (50s, graying hair, thin mustache) stands over him.

OFFICER DUPUIS
It seems that there has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother. Last night about 8 p.m. they were hit broadside. I regret to tell you that your brother is in serious condition, and your mother passed away at the scene.

Narrator drops the cup. Water spills across the linoleum.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology is pending because we think your brother may have been drunk.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Tubes and wires surround a hospital bed. 
(SOUND: Heart monitor beeps steadily)

Nick lies in the bed. His face is a canvas of black, purple, and green bruises. A plastic oxygen tube runs into his nostrils. His right leg is casted and elevated.

Narrator stands at the foot of the bed, his face a mask of cold fury.

NICK
(weakly)
Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up, man. Can't believe it. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears stream down Nick's bruised face.

NARRATOR
They took care of it. The mass was nice. She's at peace now.

NICK
I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her to church. The cops are after me now. They're telling me it could be vehicular manslaughter. Like it was all my fault.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
What do you mean? You think I meant to kill her? You think I meant to kill Ma? You son of a bitch.

Narrator stares at him. Nick is trapped, broken, unable to move.

NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

Narrator turns and walks out of the room.

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

A shiny new granite marker sits in the grass. 
(SOUND: Wind rustles the dead autumn leaves)

Narrator stands over the grave. He turns over an inverted metal vase and places a few golden mums into it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I lost my mom, and it seemed I had also lost my brother. Time crept by. The only thing that made life bearable was Sionna. But the trauma was a persistent fly that wouldn't leave us alone.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

The living room is dimly lit by a flower candle. 
(SOUND: Front door slams open against the wall)

Nick, looking scrawny and desperate, pushes Sionna hard against the hallway wall. 

SIONNA
What are you doing, Nick? Let's talk this out.

NICK
If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive right now.

Nick grabs her neck with his left hand. With his right, he pulls a switchblade and flicks it open.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight or else.

Narrator bursts through the front door.

NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Narrator lunges, grabbing Nick's arm. Nick thrashes backward. 
(SOUND: A sharp gasp as the blade hits)

The blade slices into Sionna's shoulder. Blood blooms across her shirt. She sinks to the floor.

Nick panics, elbows Narrator in the head, and sprints out the door into the night.

Narrator drops to his knees, pressing both hands hard against Sionna's bleeding shoulder.

NARRATOR
(crying)
I got you. I got you.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

Rain continues to streak the glass. 
(SOUND: Cat purrs softly on the couch)

Narrator (older, business casual) sits on the couch, petting an orange cat.

NARRATOR
She survived. But the inner wounds lingered. She couldn't feel herself anymore. She needed space, and my exit from that house was a free fall into a black, endless void. I drifted. I moved to Seattle. I built a business. But I was still in a prison of my own making.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Fluorescent lights buzz overhead. 
(SOUND: Heavy metal doors buzz and clank shut)

Narrator sits at a plexiglass divider, holding a yellow telephone receiver. 

On the other side sits Nick (30s, graying hair, scrawny, wearing an orange jumpsuit). He looks exhausted. He picks up his receiver.

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. Good to see you finally. How are you holding up?

NICK
It's not a cakewalk, but I got my routine down. Looking pretty good for getting out early. Maybe two more years.

NARRATOR
I'm here to tell you some things. And I would appreciate it if you would just listen.

Nick looks down at the table, resigned.

NICK
Yeah. Okay, man.

NARRATOR
I have spent the last eight years thinking and growing. I hope this doesn't come out too harsh, but frankly, you're an asshole. You killed my mother in a drunk driving accident. You held me down my entire life. And you tried to kill the love of my life. I'll never see you again because of you, Nick.

Nick stares at the counter.

NARRATOR
But now that you know how I feel, I have to add this bit. I forgive you. I forgive you for being a huge asshole because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Nick looks up. Tears well in his eyes and flow down his cheeks, splashing onto the hard gray surface.

NICK
(crying)
I'm so sorry. I'm so sorry. You're so right, man. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. It's all I think about. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

GUARD (O.S.)
Time's almost up. Wrap it up.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. I want you to know that after all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK
I hear you, man. I love you too. Say, what are you doing now?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle and we'll be expanding. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK
Wow, man. President Olson. That's amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

Nick smiles weakly. The Guard taps his shoulder. Nick hangs up the phone and is shuffled away down the hallway.

EXT. AIRPORT TERMINAL - DAY

Travelers rush past with rolling luggage. 
(SOUND: Intercom chimes overhead)

Narrator walks through the terminal. His posture is straight, his steps light. He smiles.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - DAY

The rain has stopped. Sunlight breaks through the clouds, illuminating the room.
(MUSIC: A gentle, uplifting acoustic melody begins)

Narrator stands by the window, looking out at the brightened city.

NARRATOR
The weight of thirty years was gone. I was free to live without fear, guilt, sadness, or judgment. His voice entered my head again. 'President Olson.' That's me. President Peter Olson.

He takes a sip of his coffee, at peace.

FADE OUT.

THE END
